# 48 MHz on the CORE clock (period 20.833333 ns) -- hardware clock, and the
# frequency this build is allowed to be reported at.
#
# Hardware chain: 27 MHz on D18 -> GTP_PPLL I=1/M=1/F=64/O0=36 -> 27 x 64/36 = 48.000 MHz.
# VCO = 27 x 64 = 1728 MHz (legal 1330..2133), PFD 27 MHz.
#
# Why there are TWO clocks below
# ------------------------------
# Measured on this netlist family (P23b/P30b/P31a/P31b/P33a/P35a):
#   * the *routed* path is a lottery with ~+-1.5 ns spread -- 45 MHz constraint
#     gave 22.94 ns, 50 MHz gave 20.14 ns, 55 MHz gave 24.14 ns (mistakes-log #68);
#   * the placer stops as soon as it meets the declared period, so declaring
#     exactly the target frequency is the worst way to ask for that frequency;
#   * re-reporting on the routed database is impossible: this PDS build has no
#     `open_db`/`load_db` (and `help` prints "successfully" even for a missing
#     command -- mistakes-log #47).
#
# So `core_clk` is the real hardware clock and is the row the frequency claim is
# read from; `core_clk_pnr` is the same physical clock declared one rung tighter
# (50 MHz) with `-add`, purely so the placer does not settle early.  Both are
# analysed, and the compliance evidence is the `core_clk` row: 48 MHz, 0 failing
# setup endpoints at 20.833 ns.  The design does NOT claim 50 MHz.
create_clock -name board_clk -period 37.037037 -waveform {0 18.5185185} [get_ports {clk}]
create_clock -name core_clk -period 20.833333 -waveform {0 10.4166665} [get_pins {u_core_pll/CLKOUT0}]
create_clock -name core_clk_pnr -period 20.000000 -waveform {0 10.000000} -add [get_pins {u_core_pll/CLKOUT0}]
